.TH WALL 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wall \- write a message to all logged-in users
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wall
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wall
reads a message from
.IR file ,
or from standard input until end of file if no
.I file
is given, and writes it to the terminal of every
.B USER_PROCESS
record in the utmp file.
Only the first 8191 bytes of the message are used.
Arguments after
.I file
are ignored.
.PP
Each copy is preceded by a banner of the form
.PP
.EX
Broadcast message from \fIuser\fP@\fIhost\fP at \fIhh\fP:\fImm\fP ...
.EE
.PP
with a leading bell character, where
.I user
is taken from
.BR getlogin (3),
or from the password entry of the real user ID.
.PP
The message is made safe for terminals before it is sent: newlines and
tabs pass through, carriage returns are dropped, other control
characters are shown as
.BI ^ X
and bytes with the high bit set as
.BI M\- X\c
\&.
.PP
A utmp line containing
.B /
or starting with a dot is skipped.
Each terminal
.I /dev/line
is opened non-blocking and without following symbolic links, and is
written only if it is a terminal; terminals that cannot be opened are
silently skipped.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message was processed, even if it could not be delivered to any
terminal.
.TP
.B 1
.I file
or the utmp file could not be opened, or memory was exhausted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Current login records.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR w (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR write (1),
.BR utmp (5)
.SH BUGS
When no terminal accepted the message,
.B wall
prints
.B "wall: no logged-in users"
even if users are logged in but their terminals could not be opened.
